    {
      "title": "Electrostatics",
      "html": "<p>Like charges repel; unlike attract. Charge is quantised: q = ne (e = 1.6\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u2079 C).</p><p><b>Coulomb's law</b>: F = kq\u2081q\u2082/r\u00b2, k = 9\u00d710\u2079 N\u00b7m\u00b2/C\u00b2.</p><p>Electric field E = F/q. Electric potential V = W/q (volt).</p><p>Capacitor: Q = CV. Energy = \u00bdCV\u00b2. Units: farad (F).</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>e = 1.6\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u2079 C.</li><li>Inverse-square law.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Charge of the electron (C):<br/><b>A:</b> 1.6\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u2079</p><p><b>Q:</b> Coulomb's law: F \u221d<br/><b>A:</b> 1/r\u00b2</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Force between +2 \u03bcC and +3 \u03bcC, 0.5 m apart.",
          "a": "F = 9\u00d710\u2079\u00b72\u00d710\u207b\u2076\u00b73\u00d710\u207b\u2076/0.25 = 0.216 N."
        },
        {
          "q": "Charge on 10 \u03bcF capacitor across 12 V?",
          "a": "Q = CV = 10\u00d710\u207b\u2076\u00b712 = 120 \u03bcC."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Current and Ohm's law",
      "html": "<p>Current I = Q/t (ampere). <b>Ohm's law</b>: V = IR (at constant T).</p><p>Resistance R = \u03c1L/A (\u03c1 = resistivity, \u03a9\u00b7m). Metals: R increases with T; semiconductors: R decreases with T.</p><p>Series: R = R\u2081+R\u2082+...; same current. Parallel: 1/R = 1/R\u2081+1/R\u2082+...; same voltage.</p><p>Electrical energy W = VIt = I\u00b2Rt = V\u00b2t/R. Power P = VI = I\u00b2R = V\u00b2/R.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Inverse-square law.</li><li>V=IR.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Coulomb's law: F \u221d<br/><b>A:</b> 1/r\u00b2</p><p><b>Q:</b> Ohm's law: V =<br/><b>A:</b> I\u00b7R</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Coulomb's law: F \u221d \u2014 1/r\u00b2.</li><li>Ohm's law: V = \u2014 I\u00b7R.</li><li>A 100 \u03a9 resistor across 10 V. Current: \u2014 0.1 A.</li><li>Power = 100 W, R = 25 \u03a9, current = \u2014 2 A.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Two resistors 4 \u03a9 and 6 \u03a9 in series across 20 V. Current?",
          "a": "R=10, I=2 A."
        },
        {
          "q": "Two 6 \u03a9 in parallel: equivalent?",
          "a": "3 \u03a9."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "EMF, internal resistance, potentiometer",
      "html": "<p>EMF (\u03b5) is the energy per unit charge supplied by a source. Terminal voltage V = \u03b5 \u2212 Ir where r is internal resistance.</p><p>A <b>potentiometer</b> measures unknown EMF by comparing balance lengths on a uniform resistance wire; no current flows through the unknown cell at balance.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Volt.</li><li>6\u22122=4.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> EMF is measured in<br/><b>A:</b> volt</p><p><b>Q:</b> A cell of EMF 6 V and internal resistance 1 \u03a9 supplies 2 A. Terminal V:<br/><b>A:</b> 4 V</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>EMF is measured in \u2014 volt.</li><li>A cell of EMF 6 V and internal resistance 1 \u03a9 supplies 2 A. Terminal V: \u2014 4 V.</li><li>A potentiometer is used to measure \u2014 EMF accurately.</li><li>An induced EMF of 6 V is produced when flux changes by 0.3 Wb. Time taken: \u2014 0.05 s.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "\u03b5=6 V, r=0.5 \u03a9 supplying 2 A. Terminal V?",
          "a": "V = 6 \u2212 2\u00b70.5 = 5 V."
        },
        {
          "q": "Advantage of a potentiometer over a voltmeter?",
          "a": "Draws no current at balance; gives true EMF."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Electrolysis and cells",
      "html": "<p>Electrolysis: passing current through an ionic solution decomposes it. Faraday's laws: (1) mass deposited m \u221d Q = It; (2) for same Q, masses \u221d chemical equivalents.</p><p>m = ZIt (Z = electrochemical equivalent).</p><p><b>Primary cells</b> (non-rechargeable): dry Leclanch\u00e9, alkaline. <b>Secondary cells</b> (rechargeable): lead-acid accumulator, Li-ion.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Charge passed.</li><li>Electrolysis.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Faraday's first law of electrolysis: m \u221d<br/><b>A:</b> Q = It</p><p><b>Q:</b> Which effect is used in electroplating?<br/><b>A:</b> chemical</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Faraday's first law of electrolysis: m \u221d \u2014 Q = It.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Z for copper = 3.3\u00d710\u207b\u2077 kg/C. Mass deposited by 2 A in 10 min?",
          "a": "m = 3.3\u00d710\u207b\u2077\u00b72\u00b7600 = 3.96\u00d710\u207b\u2074 kg."
        },
        {
          "q": "Give one secondary cell.",
          "a": "Lead-acid accumulator."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Magnetism",
      "html": "<p>Magnets have N and S poles; like repel, unlike attract. Earth acts as a giant magnet; magnetic north lies near geographic south of Earth.</p><p><b>Dip (inclination)</b>: angle between magnetic field and horizontal. <b>Declination</b>: angle between magnetic and geographic meridian.</p><p>Field lines run N \u2192 S outside a magnet.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>e = 1.6\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u2079 C.</li><li>Inverse-square law.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Charge of the electron (C):<br/><b>A:</b> 1.6\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u2079</p><p><b>Q:</b> Coulomb's law: F \u221d<br/><b>A:</b> 1/r\u00b2</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "What is dip?",
          "a": "Angle between Earth's field and horizontal."
        },
        {
          "q": "Poles of a bar magnet: like poles\u2026",
          "a": "repel."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Electromagnetism and induction; transformers",
      "html": "<p>Current in a conductor produces a magnetic field. A current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field experiences a force F = BIL sin\u03b8 (Fleming's left-hand rule).</p><p><b>Electromagnetic induction</b>: a changing magnetic flux induces an EMF. Faraday: EMF \u221d rate of change of flux. Lenz: induced current opposes the change.</p><p><b>Transformer</b>: V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p. For an ideal transformer P_in = P_out \u2192 I_p V_p = I_s V_s.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Needs changing flux.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Transformers work with<br/><b>A:</b> AC only</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Transformers work with \u2014 AC only.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Step-down transformer: N_p=1000, N_s=200, V_p=240 V. V_s?",
          "a": "V_s = 240\u00b7200/1000 = 48 V."
        },
        {
          "q": "Which rule gives the direction of the induced EMF?",
          "a": "Lenz's law (Fleming's right-hand rule)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
